{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 49",
  "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
  "worked_solution": "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
